 @bigturkey1  A 55" OLED is a TV. Not meant for PC gaming, but you can use it that way if you want. A 4k Computer monitor isn't going to be 55 inches, for obvious reasons. 

I sit within 30 inches of my monitor, and anything bigger than 30 inches would have me turning my head to view the whole screen. For me, 24 inches is the maximum screen size if I want to be able to use more than 1 monitor and also not turn my head too much. 

I have 2x 24 inch monitors, one horizontal one vertical, which gives an approximate viewing area of 30 inches. As someone trying to pixel peep, anything bigger would make 1080p begin to look bad, and they don't make a 1440p in smaller than 27 inches.

@smittyvanjagermanjenson182 This isn’t exactly true; over time newer memory standards actually often use narrower buses at higher data rates because it results in lower cost (relevant here), power consumption, and complexity overall. Very important in a laptop that has to be power efficient and has P as well as Efficiency cores etc. This is standard in industry- it’s just highlighted here because of how nvidia positions its cards price-wise. You make the mistake in thinking clock speed and bus size aren’t interchangeable and contributory to the same effective performance- for example if you halve the bus width but double the clock, your bandwidth stays the same:

512 bits * 1 GHz = 256 bits * 2 GHz

Bigger busses have more bandwidth per clock but overall bandwidth, given a certain clock-speed is actually what matters imo. If I have a card with 1/2 as much memory but running over twice as fast then it can still have comparable or even better total bandwidth, depending on the exact values.  In the case of this card, tradeoffs were made in terms of memory standard (and therefore also quantity per dollar*) versus overall clock speed. It’s very likely the busses they chose allowed them better overall performance versus other *cost-effective alternative configurations- I can guarantee engineers tested both possibilities. Maybe they got overruled by business overlings but you get my drift. Plus, at the end of the day a card with 16gb’s of slow bandwidth memory at the same clock as a card w 16gb’s of high bandwidth memory simply are useful for different things way beyond gaming, although I would also argue memory bandwidth is one of the last reasons you will see a graphics card be choked in performance in gaming use cases.

The main question is need to ask yourself is what's your budget?

Generally speaking, get at least a 12GB GPU, something along the lines of a 3060, or better. Nothing crazy, and it'll get you decent performance for more than just GTA 6. You could go below that, if your budget is lower. 

